    isbefore: []  # c7fe
    duplicate_of: []  # c7fe
    not_duplicate_of: []  # c7fe
    pr_ref:  # c7fe
    description: "The per-edge `access_mode` field defaults to `write` for several categories of read or mutation operation, producing systematic mislabeling. Edges with evidence types that explicitly indicate a read (such as `ast_name_read` or `function_reference`) carry `access_mode='write'`; module-attribute references are written as `write` on nearly every populated record; calls to collection-mutator methods are written as `write` rather than the `mutate` value the four-cell vocabulary provides. The emitter does not consult the evidence type or the call target when assigning the access-mode value, so the underlying read/write/mutate distinction is lost at write time.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) `references` edges with `evidence_type=ast_name_read` carry `access_mode='write'` — direct contradiction within the same `edge.meta`.** 1230 of 4250 `references` edges (29%) carry both `ast_name_read` and `access_mode='write'` simultaneously. Additional 852 edges with `evidence_type='function_reference'` are also tagged `access_mode='write'` (function references are reads, not writes). Combined, 2082 edges — about 49% of all `references` edges — have a direct internal contradiction. Sample edge: `src='python:conftest.py:30-36:_find_venv_dir:function'`, `dst='python:conftest.py:27-27:_REPO_ROOT:variable'`, `type='references'`, `meta={'access_mode': 'write', 'evidence_type': 'ast_name_read'}` — `_find_venv_dir` is reading `_REPO_ROOT`, not writing it. The two fields are written independently rather than derived from a shared inference.\n\n**(2) `module_attr_ref` edges labeled `access_mode='write'` on 95% of populated cases.** Of 1326 `module_attr_ref` edges, 556 have `access_mode` set; of those, 531 (95%) are `write` and only 25 are `read`. Sample `write`-tagged targets include `importlib.util`, `os.environ`, `importlib.metadata` — all of which are reads of imported attributes. The `evidence_type` is `module_attribute_reference` on all 531. Example: `'python:packages/hypergumbo/tests/test_meta.py:27-32:_load_meta'` → `'python:importlib:0-0:importlib.util:attribute'` tagged `write` — but `_load_meta` is dereferencing `importlib.util` (a read). The `module_attr_ref` emitter defaults `access_mode='write'` for any module attribute reference regardless of context; the disambiguation logic that should detect attribute assignment is either missing or inverted.\n\n**(3) `calls` to collection-mutators labeled `access_mode='write'` instead of `mutate`.** Of 2480 `calls` edges with `access_mode='write'`, the top callees are `append` (1183), `add` (282), `extend` (151), `setdefault` (86), `write` (63). These are mutation operations on existing receivers and should map to `mutate`. The `mutate` cell is used only 161 times across the whole behavior map despite roughly 1616 calls to known collection-mutator methods. The vocabulary has 4 cells (`read` / `write` / `mutate` / `delete`) but only 3 are functionally distinguished in practice — `write` is overloaded with `mutate`. Note: `event_publishes` edges have 100% `access_mode` coverage and consistently use `write` (likely correct since events ARE writes); the issue is specifically about call-edge mutators not being routed to `mutate`.\n\n### Invariant\n\nAccess-mode values should be derived from evidence type or call target — read operations labeled `read`, collection mutations labeled `mutate`, and only true assignments labeled `write`. The four-cell vocabulary (`read` / `write` / `mutate` / `delete`) exists; the emitter should use it. Two metadata fields on the same edge that encode the same underlying property (read-vs-write semantics) must agree, not contradict each other.\n\n### Scope\n\nAffects a large fraction of `references` edges (about half mass-mislabeled, 2082/4250), near-universal coverage on `module_attr_ref` edges (about 95% of populated values labeled write), and the consistent polarity error on every observed collection-mutator call across `calls` edges.\n\n### Exposure\n\nBehavior-map per-edge `access_mode` field on `calls`, `references`, and `module_attr_ref` edges; downstream consumers using `access_mode` for data-flow analysis.\n\n### Consequence\n\nAny consumer using `access_mode` to distinguish data-flow read from write gets systematically wrong results. Tooling that aggregates \"where is this variable written\" or \"what mutates this collection\" inherits the polarity error and produces incorrect analyses. The same-edge contradiction (`evidence_type=ast_name_read` + `access_mode=write`) means schema validation cannot enforce internal consistency on existing data.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- For `references` edges: derive `access_mode` from `evidence_type` at emission time. `ast_name_read` and `function_reference` → `read`; only emit `write` when the evidence type explicitly indicates an assignment.\n- For `module_attr_ref` edges: invert the default — module attribute references are reads unless context (assignment-target position) indicates write. Audit the disambiguation logic that's currently producing 95% `write`.\n- For `calls` edges: route calls to known collection-mutator methods (`append`, `add`, `extend`, `setdefault`, `write`) to `access_mode='mutate'` rather than `'write'`. Build a list of mutator methods per language.\n- Add a property test that asserts no edge carries `evidence_type='ast_name_read'` and `access_mode='write'` simultaneously; extend with similar invariants for the other observed contradictions.\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-nogib, INV-hofil, INV-fadov — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # c7fe
    fields:  # c7fe
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-2c3b8c"  # c7fe
      statement: "access-mode values should be derived from evidence type or call target — read operations labeled `read`, collection mutations labeled `mutate`, and only true assignments labeled `write`. The four-cell vocabulary (read / write / mutate / delete) exists; the emitter should use it."  # c7fe
      root_cause: "The emitter that writes a per-edge `access_mode` field defaults to the value `write` for several categories of read operation. Edges with evidence types that explicitly indicate a read (such as bare-name reads of variables, or function references) carry `access_mode='write'`. Module-attribute references — accessing attributes of imported modules without writing to them — are written as `write` on nearly every populated record. Calls to collection-mutator methods (append, add, extend, setdefault, write) are written as `write` rather than the `mutate` value the four-cell vocabulary provides for in-place modification. The emitter does not consult the evidence type or the call target when assigning the access-mode value."  # c7fe
